C++ provides comprehensive object-oriented features enabling reusable, modular, and scalable software architecture.

Core Pillars:
1. Encapsulation: Bundling data attributes and member functions inside a class while restricting direct access via private, protected, and public access specifiers.
2. Inheritance: Deriving new classes from existing base classes (class Dog : public Animal).
3. Polymorphism: Performing a single action in different ways. In C++, runtime polymorphism is achieved using virtual functions and base class pointers/references.

Virtual Functions & vtable:
class Shape {
public:
    virtual void draw() const = 0; // Pure virtual (Abstract class)
    virtual ~Shape() {} // Essential virtual destructor
};

class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    void draw() const override {
        std::cout << "Drawing Circle\n";
    }
};

Modern C++ Best Practices:
- Use override keyword explicitly to catch signature mismatches at compile time.
- Always declare base class destructors virtual to guarantee proper cleanup of derived resources.
- Prefer composition over deep inheritance hierarchies.
